['```
import java.security.MessageDigest;

public static String hashStrings(String... strings) {
  // Calculate the total length of all strings to be hashed
  int totalLength = 0;
  for (String s : strings) {
    totalLength += s.length();
  }

  // Check if the total length exceeds the maximum allowed size
  if (totalLength > 8192) {
    // Trim excess characters from each string to fit within the limit
    for (String s : strings) {
      s = s.substring(0, Math.min(s.length(), 8192));
    }
  }

  // Concatenate all strings and hash them using SHA-256
  String concatenatedString = String.join("", strings);
  MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
  byte[] hash = md.digest(concatenatedString.getBytes());

  // Convert the hash to a hexadecimal string for output
  StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
  for (byte b : hash) {
    sb.append(Integer.toString((b & 0xff) + 0x100, 16).substring(1));
  }
  return sb.toString();
}

public static void main(String[] args) {
  // Test the method with various input combinations
  String s1 = "abc";
  String s2 = "defghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz";
  String s3 = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";
  String s4 = "0123456789";
  String s5 = "";
  String s6 = "a";
  String s7 = "aaaaaaabbbbbbbccccccdddddddeeeeeeefff";

  System.out.println("hashStrings(" + s1 + ") = " + hashStrings(s1));
  System.out.println("hashStrings(" + s2 + ") = " + hashStrings(s2));
  System.out.println("hashStrings(" + s3 + ") = " + hashStrings(s3));
  System.out.println("hashStrings(" + s4 + ") = " + hashStrings(s4));
  System.out.println("hashStrings(" + s5 + ") = " + hashStrings(s5));
  System.out.println("hashStrings(" + s6 + ") = " + hashStrings(s6));
  System.out.println("hashStrings(" + s7 + ") = " + hashStrings(s7));
}
```
This code uses the `MessageDigest` class from Java's standard library to perform SHA-256 hashing on a concatenated string of input strings. The `String.join()` method is used to concatenate all strings together, and the resulting string is then hashed using `MessageDigest.digest()`.

The method first calculates the total length of all input strings to determine if they exceed the maximum allowed size of 8192 characters. If so, it trims each string to fit within the limit by taking the substring from index 0 with a length of at most 8192 using `String.substring()`.

The method then concatenates all input strings together and hashes them using SHA-256. The resulting hash is converted to a hexadecimal string for output using `Integer.toString()` and `substring()`.

To test the method, various input combinations are used, including empty strings, short strings, long strings, and strings that are near the limit of 8192 characters in length. The output of the method is printed to the console to verify that it behaves correctly under various input conditions.']

