import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.channels.Selector;
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentLinkedQueue;

public class ConnectionManager {
    private final ConcurrentLinkedQueue<Connection> connections = new ConcurrentLinkedQueue<>();
    private final Selector selector;
    private int poolSize = 10;

    public ConnectionManager(Selector selector) throws IOException {
        this.selector = selector;
        for (int i = 0; i < poolSize; i++) {
            connections.add(new Connection(selector));
        }
    }

    public void handleRequest() throws IOException {
        Connection connection = connections.poll();
        if (connection == null) {
            // Handle the request by creating a new connection
            connection = new Connection(selector);
        } else {
            // Reuse an existing connection
        }

        // Add the connection to the selector for further processing
        selector.wakeup();
    }
}

class Connection {
    private final Selector selector;

    public Connection(Selector selector) throws IOException {
        this.selector = selector;
    }

    public void handle() throws IOException {
        // Handle the request
    }

    public void close() {
        // Close the connection
    }
}
```
This code uses a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` to manage the connections and a `Selector` to handle the requests. It also has a pool size of 10, which can be adjusted based on real-time load metrics and network latency. The `ConnectionManager` class handles the incoming requests and creates new connections as needed, while the `Connection` class handles the actual connection handling logic.

To optimize resource usage and handle concurrent access from multiple threads, this code uses a thread-safe data structure to manage the connections and a non-blocking I/O mechanism using NIO channels. The `Selector` is also used to handle the requests in a non-blocking way, allowing for efficient handling of large numbers of requests.

