Here is a possible implementation of the custom asynchronous aggregation mechanism in JavaScript:
```
const asyncAggregate = (data) => {
  // Split the data into chunks based on the chunk size
  const chunkSize = 100;
  const chunks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < data.length; i += chunkSize) {
    chunks.push(data.slice(i, i + chunkSize));
  }

  // Create a promise to handle each chunk concurrently
  const promises = [];
  for (const chunk of chunks) {
    promises.push(new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      // Process the chunk independently and cohesively
      // to avoid data corruption or race conditions
      const processedChunk = processChunk(chunk);
      resolve(processedChunk);
    }));
  }

  // Wait for all chunks to be processed concurrently
  Promise.all(promises).then((results) => {
    // Aggregate the results from each chunk
    const aggregatedResults = results.reduce((acc, result) => acc + result, 0);
    console.log(`Aggregated results: ${aggregatedResults}`);
  }).catch((error) => {
    // Handle errors gracefully and log them
    console.error(error);
  });
};

// Process a single chunk of data independently
const processChunk = (chunk) => {
  const processedChunk = [];
  for (const element of chunk) {
    if (element != null) {
      processedChunk.push(element);
    }
  }
  return processedChunk;
};
```
This implementation uses a promise-based approach to handle concurrent processing of large datasets, where each chunk is processed independently and cohesively to avoid data corruption or race conditions. The function also logs the progress and errors encountered during the aggregation process.

