['Here is a sample implementation of the C++ function to initialize an octree data structure that supports dynamic expansion and efficient memory management:
```c
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>

// Define the Octree node struct
struct OctreeNode {
    // Children nodes of this node
    std::unique_ptr<OctreeNode[]> children;

    // Data associated with this node
    int data;
};

// Define the Octree class
class Octree {
public:
    // Constructor to initialize the octree with a given feature size, initial number of leaf nodes, maximum capacity, and output file path
    Octree(int featureSize, int numLeafNodes, int maxCapacity, std::string outputFilePath) {
        // Initialize the root node
        root_ = std::make_unique<OctreeNode>();

        // Set the feature size and maximum capacity
        featureSize_ = featureSize;
        maxCapacity_ = maxCapacity;

        // Set the initial number of leaf nodes
        numLeafNodes_ = numLeafNodes;

        // Open the output file for writing
        outFile_.open(outputFilePath, std::ios::out | std::ios::binary);
    }

    // Function to add a new node to the octree
    void AddNode(const int* data) {
        // Check if the node is at maximum capacity
        if (root_->children.size() == maxCapacity_) {
            // Expand the capacity of the root node by 2 times
            root_->children.resize(root_->children.size() * 2);
        }

        // Get the next available child index
        int childIndex = root_->children.size();

        // Create a new child node and set its data
        OctreeNode* newChild = new OctreeNode;
        newChild->data = data[featureSize_ * childIndex];

        // Add the new child to the octree
        root_->children.push_back(std::unique_ptr<OctreeNode>(newChild));
    }

    // Function to serialize the octree structure into a binary file
    void Serialize() {
        // Write the number of leaf nodes to the output file
        outFile_.write((char*)&numLeafNodes_, sizeof(int));

        // Write the feature size to the output file
        outFile_.write((char*)&featureSize_, sizeof(int));

        // Write the maximum capacity to the output file
        outFile_.write((char*)&maxCapacity_, sizeof(int));

        // Write the root node to the output file
        serializeNode(root_);
    }

private:
    // Function to serialize a given octree node to the binary file
    void serializeNode(const std::unique_ptr<OctreeNode>& node) {
        // Write the data of the current node to the output file
        outFile_.write((char*)&node->data, sizeof(int));

        // If the current node has children, recursively serialize them
        if (node->children.size() > 0) {
            for (const auto& child : node->children) {
                serializeNode(child);
            }
        }
    }

    // Root node of the octree
    std::unique_ptr<OctreeNode> root_;

    // Feature size of the data
    int featureSize_;

    // Maximum capacity of the octree
    int maxCapacity_;

    // Number of leaf nodes in the octree
    int numLeafNodes_;

    // Output file for serializing the octree structure
    std::ofstream outFile_;
};
```
In this implementation, the `Octree` class is defined to manage an octree data structure that supports dynamic expansion and efficient memory management. The constructor takes in the feature size, initial number of leaf nodes, maximum capacity, and output file path as parameters. It initializes the root node with an empty children vector and opens the output file for writing.

The `AddNode` function is used to add a new node to the octree. It checks if the root node is at maximum capacity and expands its capacity by 2 times if necessary. Then, it creates a new child node and sets its data to the value of the input data pointer at the current child index. The new child node is added to the octree using `push_back` method of the children vector.

The `Serialize` function serializes the octree structure into a binary file by writing the number of leaf nodes, feature size, and maximum capacity to the output file. It then recursively serializes each node in the tree using the `serializeNode` function. This function writes the data of the current node to the output file and recursively serializes its children if it has any.

The `OctreeNode` struct is defined to represent a single node in the octree, which has a vector of pointers to its child nodes and an integer representing its data value. The `root_` variable points to the root node of the octree, and the `featureSize_`, `maxCapacity_`, and `numLeafNodes_` variables are used to store the feature size, maximum capacity, and number of leaf nodes in the octree respectively.

The `outFile_` variable is an output file stream that writes the serialized octree structure to a binary file. The `std::ios::out | std::ios::binary` flag specifies that the file should be opened for writing in binary mode.']

