    record->offset = offset;
    record->value[0]++;
    record->value[1] += offset / 1000;
    record->value[2] += offset / 10000;
  }

  // Release the lock on the memory pool
  sem_post(&memory_pool.lock);
}

// Define a function to handle serialization of sorted records into a file, ensuring efficient storage and retrieval
void serialize_records(Record *records, int num_records) {
  // Open the file for writing in binary mode
  FILE *fp = fopen("output.bin", "wb");
  if (fp == NULL) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Error: Failed to open output file\n");
    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
  }

  // Write the records to the file in binary format
  for (int i = 0; i < num_records; i++) {
    fwrite(&records[i], sizeof(Record), 1, fp);
  }

  // Close the file
  fclose(fp);
}
```

This code uses a memory pool to store and manage the records. The `get_record` function checks if the record with the specified key is already present in the memory pool, and if so, it returns a pointer to that record. If not, it creates a new record and adds it to the memory pool. The `add_record` function takes a pointer to a new record as an argument and adds it to the memory pool.

The `handle_interface_failure`, `handle_feed_unavailable`, and `handle_io_error` functions are used to handle errors that may occur during data collection or processing. These functions create a new record with the specified key, set its offset value to 0, and increment the corresponding metric (e.g., `value[0]++`).

The `log_metrics` function is used to log significant changes in metrics. This function creates a new record with the specified key and sets its offset value to the current time in milliseconds. It then increments the corresponding metric (e.g., `value[0]`), `value[1]`, and `value[2]`.

The `serialize_records` function is used to serialize the sorted records into a file. This function opens the file for writing in binary mode, writes the records to it in binary format, and then closes the file.']

