To handle filenames containing spaces, special characters, and multiple dots, this function uses `strrchr` to locate the last occurrence of `/` in the file path, then increments the pointer to point to the first character after the `/`. This way, it ignores any path components and focuses only on the filename. The function then iterates through each character of the filename, counting the number of alphanumeric characters until it encounters a non-alphanumeric character or reaches the end of the string.

In addition, this function uses `snprintf` to construct the new filename by appending the number of alphanumeric characters to the original filename, ensuring that the resulting filename does not exceed 255 characters in total length, including the path, filename, and extension.

