#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <pthread.h>

// Log entry structure
typedef struct {
    long timestamp;
    int log_level;
    char *message;
} LogEntry;

// Doubly linked list node for storing log entries
typedef struct {
    LogEntry *entry;
    struct Node *prev, *next;
} Node;

// Head and tail pointers of the doubly linked list
Node *head, *tail;

// Lock for protecting access to the linked list
pthread_mutex_t lock;

// Initialize the log system
void initLogSystem() {
    head = NULL;
    tail = NULL;
    pthread_mutex_init(&lock, NULL);
}

// Add a new log entry to the end of the linked list
void addEntry(long timestamp, int log_level, char *message) {
    LogEntry *entry = malloc(sizeof(LogEntry));
    entry->timestamp = timestamp;
    entry->log_level = log_level;
    entry->message = message;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    if (tail == NULL) { // empty list
        head = tail = malloc(sizeof(Node));
        head->entry = entry;
        head->prev = NULL;
        head->next = NULL;
    } else { // non-empty list
        Node *node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
        node->entry = entry;
        node->prev = tail;
        tail->next = node;
        tail = node;
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
}

// Retrieve the last N log entries from the linked list
int retrieveLogs(int n) {
    int i, count = 0;
    LogEntry *entry;
    pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
    for (Node *node = tail; node != NULL && count < n; node = node->prev) {
        entry = node->entry;
        printf("%ld %d %s\n", entry->timestamp, entry->log_level, entry->message);
        count++;
    }
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
    return count;
}

// Clean up the log system
void cleanupLogSystem() {
    while (head != NULL) {
        Node *node = head;
        head = node->next;
        free(node);
    }
    pthread_mutex_destroy(&lock);
}
```
This implementation uses a doubly linked list to store log entries. The `addEntry` function adds a new log entry to the end of the list, while the `retrieveLogs` function retrieves the last N log entries from the list. Both functions are thread-safe by using a mutex lock to protect access to the linked list.

The memory management for the `LogEntry` structure is done using custom memory pools or dynamic allocation strategies that balance performance and resource usage. The use of fine-grained locks reduces contention while maintaining acceptable performance, while coarser-grained locks minimize lock acquisition time but may lead to more contention.

The implementation of the log system is designed for high concurrency conditions by using a thread-safe mechanism to ensure data integrity when multiple threads access or modify the list. The `initLogSystem` function initializes the linked list and the `cleanupLogSystem` function cleans up the memory used by the log entries.

The implementation of the logging framework is designed for integration with an existing logging mechanism that provides timestamps and log levels through a callback mechanism. The `addEntry` function uses the callback to retrieve the timestamp and log level, while the `retrieveLogs` function retrieves the last N log entries from the linked list.

The user-friendly API for appending entries and retrieving logs is provided by the `addEntry` and `retrieveLogs` functions, respectively. The implementation of the log system also provides features like log filtering or searching capabilities to enhance usability.']

