4. Here is an example of how you might implement a sorting algorithm in C:
```
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <stdio.h>

// Function to sort an array of integers in ascending order using a stable sorting algorithm
void stable_sort(int* arr, int n) {
  // Create a copy of the original array for swapping purposes
  int* arr2 = (int*)malloc(sizeof(int) * n);
  memcpy(arr2, arr, sizeof(int) * n);

  // Loop through each element in the array and swap it with its sorted position
  for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
    int min_idx = i;
    for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
      if (arr[j] < arr[min_idx]) {
        min_idx = j;
      }
    }
    if (min_idx != i) {
      swap(arr2, i, min_idx);
    }
  }

  // Free the memory allocated for the copy of the original array
  free(arr2);
}
```
This function first creates a copy of the original array using `malloc` and `memcpy`. It then loops through each element in the array and swaps it with its sorted position using a stable sorting algorithm. Finally, it frees the memory allocated for the copy of the original array. The sorting algorithm used is stable, meaning that the order of equal elements is preserved.

